{
  "configuration_id": "config_003",
  "reason": "With only one completed experiment (config_008: multimodal, singleprofile, pca_n2000, resnet50, concat → 52.7% acc, 0.85 GPU hrs), the evidence is very sparse and the most critical uncertainty is whether moving from singleprofile to multiprofile with aggregation meaningfully improves performance. Config_003 (multimodal, multiprofile, pca_n2000, resnet50, avgpool, concat) is the cleanest single-axis comparison to config_008: it changes only the profile setting (single→multi) and adds avgpool aggregation, keeping the RNA encoder, image encoder, and fusion method identical. This isolates the effect of multiprofile aggregation, which is likely one of the highest-impact architectural decisions in this search space. If multiprofile helps, it unlocks a large family of promising configs (002, 004, 012, 014, etc.); if it does not, we can focus budget on encoder/fusion choices within singleprofile. Expected relative cost: moderate, roughly 1.5–2.0 GPU hours (multiprofile adds per-profile forward passes but avgpool is a cheap aggregation step, and ResNet50 is the lighter image encoder), making it a cost-effective information-gathering experiment early in the budget."
}

